  "content": {
    "id": "p21-q7-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "7",
    "prompt": "Solve each of the following for $(x,y)$ using the substitution method.",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p21-q7-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$$\\begin{aligned}x+by&=c\\\\x&=by-c\\end{aligned}$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$(x,y)=\\left(0,\\frac{c}{b}\\right)$, where $b\\ne0$.\n\nSubstitute $x=by-c$ into the first equation. If $b=0$, there are no solutions unless $c=0$; then $(0,t)$ is a solution for every real $t$.",
          "worked": "Substitute $x=by-c$ into $x+by=c$.\n$$\\begin{align*}(by-c)+by&=c\\\\2by-c&=c\\\\2by&=2c\\\\y&=\\frac{c}{b}\\\\x&=b\\left(\\frac{c}{b}\\right)-c\\\\x&=0\\end{align*}$$\nThus $(x,y)=\\left(0,\\frac{c}{b}\\right)$ for $b\\ne0$.\n\nIf $b=0$, the equations require both $x=c$ and $x=-c$. There is no solution when $c\\ne0$. When $c=0$, $x=0$ and $y$ is unrestricted.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 102
      },
      {
        "id": "p21-q7-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$$\\begin{aligned}x+ay&=-c\\\\x&=ay+c\\end{aligned}$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$(x,y)=\\left(0,-\\frac{c}{a}\\right)$, where $a\\ne0$.\n\nSubstitute $x=ay+c$ into the first equation. If $a=0$, there are no solutions unless $c=0$; then $(0,t)$ is a solution for every real $t$.",
          "worked": "Substitute $x=ay+c$ into $x+ay=-c$.\n$$\\begin{align*}(ay+c)+ay&=-c\\\\2ay+c&=-c\\\\2ay&=-2c\\\\y&=-\\frac{c}{a}\\\\x&=a\\left(-\\frac{c}{a}\\right)+c\\\\x&=0\\end{align*}$$\nThus $(x,y)=\\left(0,-\\frac{c}{a}\\right)$ for $a\\ne0$.\n\nIf $a=0$, the equations require both $x=-c$ and $x=c$. There is no solution when $c\\ne0$. When $c=0$, $x=0$ and $y$ is unrestricted.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 102
      },
      {
        "id": "p21-q7-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$$\\begin{aligned}y-ax&=b\\\\y&=bx+a\\end{aligned}$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$(x,y)=(1,a+b)$, where $a\\ne b$.\n\nSubstitute $y=bx+a$ into the first equation. If $a=b$, every pair $(t,at+a)$ is a solution.",
          "worked": "Substitute $y=bx+a$ into $y-ax=b$.\n$$\\begin{align*}(bx+a)-ax&=b\\\\(b-a)x+a&=b\\\\(b-a)x&=b-a\\\\x&=1\\\\y&=b(1)+a\\\\y&=a+b\\end{align*}$$\nThus $(x,y)=(1,a+b)$ for $a\\ne b$.\n\nIf $a=b$, both equations describe $y=ax+a$, so there are infinitely many solutions $(t,at+a)$, where $t$ is real.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 92
      },
      {
        "id": "p21-q7-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "$$\\begin{aligned}y+ax&=-b\\\\y&=bx+a\\end{aligned}$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$(x,y)=(-1,a-b)$, where $a\\ne-b$.\n\nSubstitute $y=bx+a$ into the first equation. If $a=-b$, every pair $(t,bt+a)$ is a solution.",
          "worked": "Substitute $y=bx+a$ into $y+ax=-b$.\n$$\\begin{align*}(bx+a)+ax&=-b\\\\(a+b)x+a&=-b\\\\(a+b)x&=-(a+b)\\\\x&=-1\\\\y&=b(-1)+a\\\\y&=a-b\\end{align*}$$\nThus $(x,y)=(-1,a-b)$ for $a\\ne-b$.\n\nIf $a=-b$, both equations describe $y=bx+a$, so there are infinitely many solutions $(t,bt+a)$, where $t$ is real.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 92
      }
    ]
  },
